### Exercise 2.5 — Removal inventory

**Goal**: use three removal APIs for their three different contracts.

**Info**: `pop` takes from the end (`Option<T>`), `remove(i)` shifts the tail and returns the element, `drain(range)` removes a run and yields it.

**Objectives**

- `pop` the last element and return it as `Option<i32>` (leave `data` shortened)
- then (in the same body) `remove` index 0 from what remains, discarding it
- keep the signature unchanged; `data` must end as `vec![20]`

**See**: §2.5 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: the body is three statements; the checks inspect both the return value and `data`.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
/// Pop the tail, drop the head, leave exactly `[20]` behind.
///
/// TODO: replace `todo!()` with pop-then-remove.
fn pop_stack_drain(data: &mut Vec<i32>) -> Option<i32> {
    todo!()
}

// --- check (do not modify) ---
let mut data = vec![10, 20, 30];
assert_eq!(pop_stack_drain(&mut data), Some(30));
assert_eq!(data, vec![20]);
assert_eq!(pop_stack_drain(&mut Vec::new()), None);
println!("Exercise 2.5 passed");
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="markdown">
### Exercise 2.6 — Order the three lines

**Goal**: arrange code so a borrow ends before the mutation it would clash with.

**Info**: NLL ends a borrow at its **last use**. A read before the push compiles; the same read *after* the push is E0502. The fix is ordering, not cloning.

**Objectives**

- implement `read_after_push`: return the length **after** pushing `extra` onto `data`
- the body must not clone and must not need two lookups
- keep the signature unchanged

**See**: §2.6 and §11.2 of the example notebook.

**Hint**: there is nothing to fix if you never hold a borrow across the push.
</VSCode.Cell>
<VSCode.Cell language="rust">
